From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hero Siege Offline Toolkit <noreply@example.invalid>
Date: Sat, 19 Sep 2026 12:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/7] runner interface: keep the init dump out of the log
 unless asked

Why: On every launch of Hero Siege, upstream's GmpRunnerInterfaceHook writes the register state, every instruction of the interface chain and all 512 rows of its emulated stack into YYToolkit.log. That is nearly the whole log a player attaches to a bug report, and the lines that matter are buried under it. The dump now needs YYTK_RI_VERBOSE=1 in the environment of the game; without it one "RI init summary:" line reports the instruction count, the emulated stores, the stack rows and how many of them are non-zero, the interface offset, whether the chain ended where it should, and which of three answers the environment gave for YYTK_RI_VERBOSE (not set / set but not 1 / 1) together with the length of its value.

Evidence: evidence/fresh-build-crash.YYToolkit.log, counted with grep: 736 lines in total, of which 7 register lines, 202 instruction lines, 2 stack pointer lines and 512 stack rows (723 lines), 487 of the 512 rows all zeros; 100 of the instructions are stores to [rsp|rbp+disp] and the chain ends on "lea rcx, [rsp+0x60]". So the expected summary on that build is 202 instructions, 100 stores, 512 rows of which 25 are non-zero, rsp+0x60. The dump is upstream code (v4.0.1 Generic-RunnerInterfaceNew.cpp:72-104, :134, :222-237) and runs once per launch, so it is log noise and NOT the in-play lag - this patch makes no performance claim. YYToolkit/hs-tests/ri_dump_test.cpp: 39 checks pass, also under /fsanitize=address; 4 single-line mutations of the gate (no trimming, a looser length test, the fit test off by one, not-set reported as refused) each fail at least one check. NOT verified: no launch of the game was made with this patch, so neither the summary numbers above nor the return of the 723 lines under YYTK_RI_VERBOSE=1 (the positive control for the gate) have been seen live; whether the launcher hands its environment on to the game. MEASURED on this host: "set NAME=1 && set NAME" in a cmd script prints the value as "1 " with the trailing blank, which is the case the trimming is for.

Fails-safe: Only the value 1 enables the dump, with blanks and tabs around it ignored (cmd's "set YYTK_RI_VERBOSE=1 && game.exe" stores "1 "); unset, empty, any other or an overlong value leaves it off. The summary line is written in every case and tells the three apart - "not set", "set, but to something other than 1" with the length of the value, or "1" - so a value that arrived and was refused cannot be mistaken for a launcher that does not hand its environment on. It also names a chain that did NOT end on the rsp-relative lea, so a broken walk is still visible without the dump. The two always-on upstream lines ("Found RSP-relative LEA", "Failed to calculate RIP-relative address") are untouched. The variable is read once per hook run with a 16 character stack buffer: no cached static, no lock, no allocation. Nothing about what the hook emulates or copies has changed - only what it logs.

Log-markers: "RI init summary:"
Log-markers: "YYTK_RI_VERBOSE is"
Log-markers: "the dump stays off"

Upstream-status: not submitted. Upstream v4.0.1 writes the dump unconditionally.

